!!! 2026-08-29 — PARTIALLY INVALIDATED. READ THIS BEFORE USING ANY NUMBER BELOW.
!!!
!!! This record was scored through rav1d-safe a6a7e232f9cd on an Apple M4 Pro,
!!! i.e. on aarch64 on the WRONG SIDE of the aarch64 NEON conformance campaign
!!! of 2026-08-07/08. At a6a7e232 that decoder reproduced 302 of 766
!!! dav1d-test-data vectors and MISMATCHED the other 464; after the campaign it
!!! is 766/766 (rav1d-safe benchmarks/aarch64_md5_fixes_2026-08-07.meta, a
!!! set-diff by vector name, 0 newly-failing at every intermediate step). The
!!! defects were real arithmetic errors in the NEON kernels — itx 16x16 per-type
!!! eob thresholds and the 16x32/32x16 identity roundings (rav1d-safe@ef01e85),
!!! and two mc compound families. The itx family sits on the transform path an
!!! intra-only still image DOES use.
!!!
!!! WHAT IS INVALID — every column whose value passes through the decoder:
!!!   * ssim2                                   (benchmarks/gate_flip_cells_2026-08-06.tsv.zst)
!!!   * bd_rate_pct                             (this file's .tsv)
!!!   * bytes_ratio_at_ssim2_{30,50,70,90}      (this file's .tsv)
!!! bd_rate_pct and the bytes_ratio_* columns are NOT a decoder-independent
!!! "bytes half": see METRIC below — they are bytes integrated/interpolated
!!! OVER THE ssim2 AXIS, so they inherit that axis's error. Both arms were
!!! decoded by the same wrong decoder, but the error is bitstream-dependent and
!!! the two arms are different bitstreams, so it does not cancel. Do NOT compare
!!! any of these five columns cell-for-cell against a post-2026-08-11 run.
!!!
!!! WHAT REMAINS VALID — nothing here is decoder-derived:
!!!   * bytes, bpp, sha256, enc_ms              (cells)
!!!   * cells, sha_identical                    (summary)
!!!   * the whole BYTE-CHANGE LIVENESS table below
!!!   * the SOLO ENCODE COST table below
!!! Measured, not assumed: rav1d-safe a6a7e232 -> 66f58fa6 leaves bytes, bpp and
!!! sha256 identical on all 1200 cells of every arm — nothing in the range
!!! touches the encode side (benchmarks/rav1d_pin_66f58fa6_2026-08-29.tsv.meta,
!!! RESULT 3).
!!!
!!! THE 10-BIT HDR TAIL SECTION IS ALSO VALID, and that is measured too, not
!!! inferred: examples/hdr_pq10_probe prints the same line (q95 s8, max |D| 1281,
!!! mean |D| 47, 1127 bytes) at a6a7e232 and at 66f58fa6 — the 10-bit path does
!!! not move anywhere across the range (same record, RESULT 5). The campaign's
!!! 16bpc repair is in the compound/prep MC kernels, which an intra-only still
!!! never reaches.
!!!
!!! SIZE OF THE ERROR, measured on this same host and corpus: re-decoding a
!!! 1200-cell grid at a6a7e232 vs the post-campaign pin moved 699 ssim2 values
!!! (58.2%), mean |delta| 0.039, max |delta| 0.610 ssim2 points, 402 higher /
!!! 297 lower — rounding-shaped, not a quality trend. Bisected, the entire
!!! movement is in a6a7e232 -> 140f9145; 140f9145 -> 66f58fa6 moved 0 of 400.
!!! So the five invalid columns are wrong by a small but non-zero and
!!! non-cancelling amount, not by a constant offset you can correct out.
!!!
!!! THIS FILE IS NOT SUPERSEDED AND MUST NOT BE DELETED: it is still the only
!!! record of the byte-level half of the 2026-08-06 gate flip, and of the
!!! encode-cost and 10-bit tables. What it needs is an ssim2 baseline
!!! re-measured on the current pin before the next gate flip, not removal.
!!!
!!! Corroborating record: benchmarks/rav1d_pin_66f58fa6_2026-08-29.tsv{.zst,.meta}.
!!! Filename note: this file is named gate_flip_summary_2026-08-06.tsv.meta.
!!! The "gate_flip_composed" on the next line is the run's title, not a filename;
!!! no file of that name has ever existed.

gate_flip_composed / 2026-08-06
Cashing in the zenrav1e dep-bump release gates: a before/after of the ravif
speed table with the gate consts off (baseline) vs on.

WHAT MOVED
  Four consts in ravif/src/av1encoder.rs flipped false -> true AND their apply
  blocks in speed_settings() uncommented (a flipped const over a commented
  apply block is a no-op that looks done):
      S1_DEEP_ARMS_LIVE      speed 1
      S6_TX_SIZE_RDO_LIVE    speeds 6-8
      S6_PART_PRUNE_LIVE     speeds 4-8
      S10_RETIER_LIVE        speeds 9-10
  S6_INTRA7_LIVE (speeds 6-8) is measured here but NOT armed — see below.
  Nothing in zenrav1e was changed; every knob these arms drive is a
  caller-armed, default-off library opt-in by that crate's own design.

ARMS IN THE FILES
  baseline_all_gates_off   all five consts false  (= the pre-flip shipped code)
  shipped_4_gates_on       the four above true, S6_INTRA7_LIVE false  (SHIPPED)
  all5_gates_on            all five true          (isolates intra7 vs shipped)

PROVENANCE
  ravif           54d1aef (this repo, branch feat/zenrav1e-master-bump)
                  code state c69050a; 54d1aef adds only docs + the git-rev
                  dev-dep pin, encoder bytes unchanged between them
  zenrav1e        4d1ed620703d7eb94bb24467b44c5b498896af62  (master, 0.2.0)
  rav1d-safe      a6a7e232f9cdbf9c4baed393738fde7fb683afc8  (decode side only)
  fast-ssim2      0.8.2 (registry)   zenresize 0.3.1 (registry)
  rustc           1.97.1 (8bab26f4f 2026-07-14)
  host            Apple M4 Pro, arm64, macOS 26.5.2, 12 cores (8P + 4E)
  corpus          ~/work/zen/codec-corpus (clic2025/training, gb82-sc)

HARNESS
  examples/gate_sweep.rs in this repo. Per cell: encode RGB8 -> AVIF with
  zenravif (defaults: 4:4:4, BT.601 full range, auto bit depth = 10-bit,
  single-threaded), parse the container with avif-parse, decode the primary
  item with rav1d-safe (1 thread, max_frame_delay 1), invert full-range BT.601
  to RGB8, score SSIMULACRA2 against the source. Emits bytes, bpp, ssim2,
  the AVIF file's sha256, and encode wall time.

  Correctness pin: every q100 cell scores exactly 100.0000, i.e. the decode
  path and the BT.601 inverse are exact. Cells are joined pre/post on
  (class, size, speed, quality); an identical sha256 means the encoder
  produced the same bytes.

  Sources were downscaled with zenresize Mitchell-Netravali. Upscaling is
  never done: a size tier above a source's native long edge is skipped.

  Build/run: cargo build --release --example gate_sweep   (RUSTC_WRAPPER=sccache,
  -j 4, no -C target-cpu=native). Exact sweep commands are in COMMANDS below.

GRID (sweep discipline: 4 size tiers, low-q density equal to high-q, 5 content
classes, every speed row a gate touches plus untouched controls)
  grid       sizes(long edge)  speeds      quality           cells/arm
  main       64, 256, 1024     4,5,6,7,8,9,10   5..100 step 5    2100
  large2048  2048              4,6,8,10         5..95  step 10    200
  s1_small   64, 256           1,2              5..100 step 5     400
  s1_med     1024              1,2              5..95  step 10    100
  Total 7,600+ encode+score cells across the three arms.

  Content classes (one source each):
    photo_a     clic2025/training/0c49a5cce349020bbba2f97ae41e90ba.png 2048x1358
    photo_b     clic2025/training/100a02c269c5948392f283b2aa3bb4da.png 2048x1365
    screen      gb82-sc/imac_dark.png                                 2940x1912
    text_ui     gb82-sc/codec_wiki.png                                2560x1664
    lineart_ui  gb82-sc/windows.png                                   2560x1392

DEVIATIONS FROM THE SWEEP-DISCIPLINE DEFAULTS, stated rather than hidden
  * The "large" tier is 2048, not 4096. No >=4096 source exists in the local
    corpus checkout for the photographic classes (clic2025 tops out at 2048)
    and upscaling is banned, so 4096 was NOT MEASURED rather than faked.
  * The 2048 and s1_med grids use quality step 10 instead of 5, and 2048 omits
    speeds 5, 7 and 9. Cost: s1 at 2048 is ~48 s/cell before the flip and
    several times that after. Density is uniform across the q range in every
    grid — no grid is denser at high q than low q.
  * s1 at 2048 was NOT MEASURED at all (same cost reason). The s1 arm is
    covered at 64, 256 and 1024.
  * Speeds 3 and 5/7 at 2048 were not swept; s2 and s3 are the untouched
    control rows and are covered in the liveness grid below.

METRIC
  Pareto-front SSIMULACRA2 BD-rate, per (content class, size, speed) curve:
  cells are reduced to a monotone rate/quality front (dominated cells dropped),
  log-rate is integrated over the overlapping ssim2 range, and the result is
  reported as a percentage. NEGATIVE = fewer bytes at matched quality = better.
  bytes_ratio_at_ssim2_N columns are the arm's byte count divided by the
  baseline's at that interpolated quality (<1 = smaller); blank where a curve
  does not span that quality.

  Caution for anyone re-deriving these: a naive BD over quality-sorted cells
  gives badly wrong answers here, because these RD curves are non-monotone at
  the low-q end. The pareto reduction is what makes them comparable.

