10-BIT HDR TAIL — zenavif tests/hdr_roundtrip.rs::pq10_pixel_fidelity_within_bounds
Reported red on the armed tree. REPRODUCED, ATTRIBUTED, and characterised here
with examples/hdr_pq10_probe.rs, which mirrors that test exactly inside this
repo (same 64x48 fixture, same encode_raw_planes_10_bit + MC=Identity GBR path,
same q95/s8, same 16->10 `v>>6` / 10->16 `(v<<6)|(v>>4)` reconstruction, same
statistic). The probe reproduces the documented baseline number 607/mean 50
bit-for-bit, so it is a faithful stand-in.

  arms armed at s8            max |D|   mean |D|   bytes
  none (pre-flip baseline)        607         50    1407
  S6_PART_PRUNE_LIVE only         960         49    1393
  S6_TX_SIZE_RDO_LIVE only       1281         47    1133
  all four (shipped)             1281         47    1127
  S1_DEEP / S10_RETIER only       607         50    1407   (inert at s8)

ATTRIBUTION: S6_TX_SIZE_RDO_LIVE is the dominant cause (607 -> 1281 on its own);
S6_PART_PRUNE_LIVE moves it to 960 on its own, which is also over the test's 900
budget. S1_DEEP and S10_RETIER do not apply at speed 8 and are exactly inert.

IS IT A DEFECT OR AN RD REALLOCATION? The evidence says reallocation:
  * Exactly ONE cell of 9,216 exceeds 900: (x=0, y=35) blue, 65535 -> 64254.
    That is 1023 -> 1003 in ten-bit units on ONE channel of an isolated
    single-pixel max-white specular impulse on a dark background — the most
    expensive thing in the fixture to code. Second worst is 772.
  * The error field keeps its structure. Per region, baseline -> shipped:
    ramp max 461 -> 461, patches max 420 -> 420 (IDENTICAL), dark+specular
    max 607 -> 1281 but its MEAN improves 95 -> 87. Only the impulse tail moved.
  * Mean fidelity improves, and at EVERY quality:
      q          70    80    85    90    95    99
      mean base 204   132   109    85    50    30
      mean ship 151    95    93    72    47    29
  * Bytes drop 18-25% at every quality:
      bytes base 835   949  1017  1125  1407  2229
      bytes ship 682   764   799   885  1127  1842
  * The max is NOT monotonically worse: base/ship max is 5157/5765 (q70),
    3075/4420 (q80), 2186/2370 (q85), 1665/1537 (q90 — ship WINS),
    607/1281 (q95), 221/240 (q99).
  * Decisive framing — compare at matched BYTES, not at a matched quality
    *setting*: baseline q90 = 1125 bytes, max 1665, mean 85.
                shipped  q95 = 1127 bytes, max 1281, mean 47.
    At the same bitrate the shipped encoder's tail is BETTER (1281 vs 1665) and
    its mean is nearly half. The test pins a quality setting whose byte cost the
    encoder no longer pays; the 900 budget was calibrated against 1407 bytes of
    output and the arms now produce that quality setting in 1127.
  * Nothing here resembles the failure shapes the test's own comment calls out:
    a channel swap is ~56000 and a plane rotation is full-scale.
  * Speed profile (q95, shipped): s2 487, s3 551, s4 487, s6 1281, s8 1281,
    s9 1281, s10 708. The spike is confined to the depth-1 intra tx-size-RDO
    band, which is exactly where the attributed arm applies.

LIMITS OF THIS EVIDENCE, stated plainly:
  * n = 1 synthetic 64x48 fixture. This is the only 10-bit content measured.
    The four arms' entire fitting record — theirs and the re-measurement above —
    is 8-bit YCbCr; a 10-bit identity-matrix (GBR planes, no chroma decorrelation)
    encode is a different regime and has now been sampled exactly once.
  * No butteraugli, and no perceptual metric at all on the 10-bit path — only
    per-channel absolute delta.
  * The budget question (is 900 still the right number, given the arms make q95
    a ~1127-byte operating point rather than a ~1407-byte one?) is NOT decided
    here. The test threshold was NOT touched, nothing was #[ignore]d, and no
    assertion was weakened. It stays red and truthful pending the user's call.

