LIVENESS OF EACH FLIPPED GATE (the specific failure mode this guards: a flipped
const over a still-commented apply block is a no-op that looks done)
  Read the table above. A row with 0/N sha-identical means every cell's bytes
  moved, i.e. the apply block reached the encoder.
    S1_DEEP_ARMS_LIVE   s1: LIVE at 256 (1/100 identical) and 1024 (0/50).
                        INERT at 64 (100/100 identical) — the deep partition
                        levers have nothing to reach on a sub-superblock frame.
    S6_PART_PRUNE_LIVE  s4,s5: LIVE (16/100 and 5/100 identical at 64, 0/100
                        everywhere else). s6-s8 also carry it.
    S6_TX_SIZE_RDO_LIVE s6-s8: LIVE (0/100 identical at every size).
    S10_RETIER_LIVE     s9,s10: LIVE (0/100 identical at every size).
    S6_INTRA7_LIVE      s6-s8 only, proven by the intra7_isolated comparison:
                        exactly those three rows move, all others 100/100
                        identical. (Measured, positive, but NOT armed — see
                        the const doc; the block is a test-premise conflict,
                        not an RD result.)
  Controls: s2 and s3 are touched by no gate and are byte-identical
  (s2 100/100 at 64 and 256, 50/50 at 1024). A separate 150-cell liveness grid
  (5 classes x 256 px x speeds 1-10 x q{30,60,90}) confirmed s3 15/15 identical.

  Separately verified that uncommenting the apply blocks while leaving all five
  consts false is byte-identical to the previously shipped commented-out code:
  50/50 sha-identical at q60 across s1-s10. That is what makes the "baseline"
  arm here a valid stand-in for the pre-flip build.

WHAT IS *NOT* IN THIS BEFORE/AFTER
  * The zenrav1e dep bump itself. Both arms are built against the same
    zenrav1e (master 4d1ed620); only the ravif consts differ. Everything
    measured here is attributable to the arms.
  * Per-SB delta-q / Variance Boost / the segmentation-disable that rides with
    them. ravif never sets `variance_boost_*` and never selects
    `Tune::Ssimulacra2`, and none of the flips touch the `segmentation` field —
    verified by inspection of the diff and of every write to those fields. So
    the "turning delta-q on also disables segmentation" step (independently
    measured elsewhere at ~+1.10 zensim / +2.9% bytes at unchanged quantizer)
    is NOT sitting inside these numbers.
  * Butteraugli. Only SSIMULACRA2 was computed. The original per-arm records
    carry butteraugli-3n / butteraugli-max vetoes; those were NOT re-checked
    here, so a metric-gaming regression would not have been caught by this
    sweep. That is a real gap, stated rather than papered over.
  * Alpha. Every source is opaque; the alpha (Cs400) encode these speed rows
    also govern is unmeasured.
  * Any size above 2048, and s1 above 1024.

COMMANDS (run from the repo root; sccache on, -j 4, no target-cpu=native)
  cargo build --release --example gate_sweep
  # per arm, with the consts set accordingly and the binary snapshotted:
  gate_sweep --label <arm> --out main_<arm>.tsv    --corpus ../codec-corpus \
      --sizes 64,256,1024 --speeds 4,5,6,7,8,9,10 --qmin 5 --qmax 100 --qstep 5
  gate_sweep --label <arm> --out large_<arm>.tsv   --corpus ../codec-corpus \
      --sizes 2048 --speeds 4,6,8,10 --qmin 5 --qmax 95 --qstep 10
  gate_sweep --label <arm> --out s1small_<arm>.tsv --corpus ../codec-corpus \
      --sizes 64,256 --speeds 1,2 --qmin 5 --qmax 100 --qstep 5
  gate_sweep --label <arm> --out s1med_<arm>.tsv   --corpus ../codec-corpus \
      --sizes 1024 --speeds 1,2 --qmin 5 --qmax 95 --qstep 10

NICE / QoS DISCIPLINE (macOS)
  Builds and test runs used `nice -n 19` (shared machine). NOTHING TIMED DID.
  On Darwin `nice` maps to background QoS and parks the process on E-cores,
  which distorts wall time by roughly 40x — it is a measurement trap, not a
  politeness knob. The RD sweeps above ran without nice but MULTI-PROCESS
  (up to 6 concurrent single-threaded encoders), so their `enc_ms` column is
  contended and usable only as a within-run ratio. The timing table below is a
  separate solo run: one process at a time, nothing else on the box, no nice.

